{#
  This node's VXLAN tunnel endpoint address.

  Rendered for EVERY node in the cluster, not just the fabric nodes. The VTEP
  CR checks the whole cluster: a node with no address inside its CIDRs never
  gets the k8s.ovn.org/vteps annotation, and one unannotated node fails the
  entire CR -

    node master1 is missing the k8s.ovn.org/vteps annotation
    ...
    reason: AllocationFailed

  - so the masters need an address even though no tenant pod ever runs on one
  (workload.yaml.j2 selects workers, which is where the fabric NICs are).
  Their VTEPs are never a next hop for any tenant route and leaf1 carries no
  route to them; they exist so the CR can be accepted.

  The VTEP CR runs in Unmanaged mode, and this policy is what that mode
  requires: OVN-Kubernetes discovers an address already on the node, it
  never creates one. Managed mode, which would allocate addresses itself, is
  not implemented as of OpenShift 4.22 and 4.23 (see vtep.yaml.j2), so on a
  real fabric too, this policy or an equivalent is how every node gets its
  VTEP address.

  It also makes the addressing deterministic - a known address on a known
  node - so leaf1 can carry static /32s written at render time. This lab's
  fabric is three FRR containers with no IGP, and something has to give
  leaf1 a route to each VTEP.

  The octet is the node's own last octet on {{ lab_network_prefix }}.0/24,
  taken from what the cluster reports as its InternalIP rather than from
  ip_list. Same scheme, same numbers - worker1 is .34 on both - but it needs
  no table of nodes to be kept in step with the cluster, which is what let
  the masters go missing here in the first place.

  A dummy interface rather than an address on the fabric NIC: the VTEP
  address is the node's identity in the overlay, not an interface address,
  and it must stay up and reachable independently of any one link. This is
  the same shape as a router loopback and is what OVN-Kubernetes expects to
  discover. It is also why this works on a master, which has no fabric NIC
  to put an address on.

  Exactly one address per node may match the VTEP CR's CIDRs - if a node has
  two, the VTEP CR goes to a failed status rather than picking one.
#}
apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1
kind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy
metadata:
  name: vtep-{{ vtep_node.name }}
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/hostname: {{ vtep_node.name }}
  desiredState:
    interfaces:
      - name: {{ udn_bgp_vtep_iface }}
        type: dummy
        state: up
        ipv4:
          enabled: true
          dhcp: false
          address:
            - ip: {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.{{ vtep_node.octet }}
              prefix-length: 32
        ipv6:
          enabled: false
